[00:08.56]One day Suzie McCash went shopping with her mum in Tynemouth, England.
[00:16.76]Suzie was four years old.
[00:20.02]Her mum didn’t feel very well at the shops, so they went home.
[00:25.56]In the house, Suzie’s mum went to lie on the sofa.
[00:30.70]She was there for a long time.
[00:33.66]Her eyes were shut and it wasn’t easy for her to breathe.
[00:38.30]Suzie was worried.
[00:40.24]Was her mum really ill?
[00:43.40]Suzie went to the house next door, but the neighbours weren’t there.
[00:48.65]So she phoned 999 – the emergency telephone number for the police.
[00:55.62]The police officer was very kind and Suzie was calm.
[01:01.52]‘What’s your mummy doing now?’
[01:04.72]‘Um, she’s just sitting on the sofa and she’s doing nothing.’
[01:10.54]‘Are her eyes open?’
[01:12.74]‘No, they’re shut.’
[01:14.56]And later…
[01:17.10]‘Can you open the door?
[01:19.48]Can you be a big girl?’
[01:22.06]Soon the police were at their house and an ambulance was there, too.
[01:27.90]About an hour later, Suzie’s mum was OK again.
[01:32.52]A doctor said: ‘Her mummy was very sick.
[01:36.72]Without Suzie’s phone call, her mummy might be dead now.’
[01:41.66]Suzie’s story was in the newspapers and people were surprised and happy about it.
[01:47.60]She was a very brave girl and, a year later, she was in the news again because she was named a Child of Courage at the Pride of Britain Awards for helping her mother.
